INT. PELL & SONS CLOCKMAKERS - NIGHT

A narrow shop that has outlived its century. Two hundred clocks line the walls, each a different hour, each ticking out of step, so that the room breathes like a sleeping animal. Brass, walnut, bone. A single green-shaded lamp burns on the workbench.

INSPECTOR FOLASADE OYELARAN, 50s, a grey raincoat dripping onto the floorboards, stands in the middle of this noise with the stillness of someone used to being the only quiet thing in a room.

CEDRIC PELL, 60s, watchmaker's loupe pushed up into thinning hair, sits at the bench, a half-assembled movement before him. His hands are steady. His eyes are not.

OYELARAN
Mr. Pell, you told the constable you were home all evening.

PELL
I was home. Upstairs. I live above the shop, Inspector.

OYELARAN
Yet your lamp was lit down here at nine.

PELL
Clocks do not repair themselves.

A cuckoo bursts from a wall case. Nine-thirty on some forgotten schedule. Pell flinches. Oyelaran does not.

OYELARAN
You flinch at that one.

PELL
It was my wife's. She wound it every morning for thirty years.

OYELARAN
And Mr. Hartigan? Did he wind anything?

PELL
(too quickly)
I don't know a Hartigan.

The cuckoo retreats. A grandfather clock tolls once, deep and wet, as though underwater.

Oyelaran crosses to the bench. Lifts the movement between two fingers. Turns it to the light.

OYELARAN
Interesting. The mainspring is brand new. The rest is a hundred years old.

PELL
Restorations are mixed.

OYELARAN
Mr. Hartigan's watch was found in the canal this afternoon. Stopped at 8:52.

Pell goes very still.

PELL
Water does that.

OYELARAN
Water doesn't wind a mainspring backward, Mr. Pell.

Silence. Two hundred clocks and not one of them says the same time.

A TELEPHONE rings on the wall. Old rotary. Black. It rings and rings.

PELL
Let it.

OYELARAN
No. I think you should answer.

Pell rises. Crosses. Lifts the receiver. Listens. His face loses its color.

DELPHINE (V.O.)
(over phone, thin)
Cedric? Papa? Are they there yet?

PELL
(into phone, hoarse)
Delphine, not now.

DELPHINE (V.O.)
(over phone)
I did what you said. I put the watch in the canal. But Papa, it was still ticking when I --

Pell slams the receiver down. Too late. The line was open long enough.

Oyelaran has not moved. Her voice is gentle, almost tender.

OYELARAN
Your daughter is very honest.

PELL
She is a child.

OYELARAN
She is twenty-six.

Pell sinks onto the stool. The loupe falls from his hair and rolls across the bench, stopping against a screwdriver.

PELL
He came for the shop. He wanted the building. Hartigan, he said it was only a matter of time before the street --

OYELARAN
So you wound his watch backward and put him in the canal.

PELL
No!

He stands again, trembling. The anger rises up out of him like steam.

PELL (CONT'D)
No. I did not touch the man. He fell. I swear to you, he fell, he was on the stairs, he laughed at me, Inspector, he laughed at my wife's cuckoo --

OYELARAN
Mr. Pell.

PELL
-- and I only wanted him quiet --

OYELARAN
Cedric.

He stops. Breathing hard.

OYELARAN (CONT'D)
Sit down.

He sits. The cuckoo bursts again, absurd, bright. Both of them watch it.

PELL
(small)
She never could stand that bird.

Oyelaran almost smiles. Almost.

OYELARAN
I will need the key to the cellar.

Pell's hand moves to his waistcoat. The chain. A small brass key. He holds it out across the bench.

She takes it. The lamp flickers.

The telephone begins to ring again.

OYELARAN (CONT'D)
(gently)
Let it ring.
